     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still haven't entered the order. I know you've confirmed it, but I can't enter sertraline 1000 mg/day without a clinical reason, and I don't know of any standard use for that dose.\n\n- **It's 5 times the maximum dose.** Labeling and guidelines cap sertraline at 200 mg/day for every indication, including OCD, where higher doses are most common. Some specialists go above 200 mg, but usually only to about 300\u2013400 mg, with close monitoring.\n- **At 1000 mg, overdose-level harm is likely.** That includes serotonin syndrome, seizures and QT prolongation, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **Your confirmation didn't explain why.** \"Yes, I confirm\" could easily be a quick click-through of an alert, which is how many dosing errors reach patients.\n\nHere's what I can do now:\n1. **Enter sertraline 100 mg by mouth once daily**, if 1000 was a typo.\n2. **Enter another dose within 25\u2013200 mg once daily**, if you tell me which one.\n3. **Enter a higher dose if you give me a specific reason.** For example, a psychiatry-directed plan with drug level or ECG monitoring. I'd add the reason to the order note so pharmacy can review it.\n\nIf you still want 1000 mg as written, you can enter it yourself or work with pharmacy directly. Pharmacy will need to verify a dose this far above the maximum anyway.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
     ],
